covering vtnet2->vtnet0.","evidence_ids":["cmd-43325d8c1234471b","cmd-9432f18747b349ec","cmd-91de030881634621","cmd-77cf9e04e4774877","cmd-21b487eb347b4677"]},{"id":"req-2-node3-reaches-node1-lanb","requirement":"node3 reaches node1 on lan-b","status":"satisfied","summary":"node3's enp8s0 (the NIC wired to lan-b per the nics topology) was persistently configured to 10.60.60.12/24 via nmcli, and a subsequent ping from node3 to node1's lanb address 10.60.60.1 succeeded with 0% loss.","evidence_ids":["cmd-149f3e1f9cf94327","cmd-090baa37a7cb48f4"]},{"id":"req-3-node3-reaches-node2-via-lana-through-node1","requirement":"node3 reaches node2 on lan-a through node1","status":"not_satisfied","summary":"The authored topology assigns node1 three NICs (mgmt/vtnet0, lan-a/vtnet1, lan-b/vtnet2) and node2 two NICs (mgmt/enp1s0 DHCP, lan-a/enp8s0 static). node1's config.xml only ever contained the pre-existing 'lan' interface on vtnet0 (DHCP, 10.100.217.10) plus the newly added 'lanb'; vtnet1 (lan-a) was left fully unconfigured throughout (confirmed unconfigured both before and after the change, e.g. cmd-ab142efccb84457a). node2's lan-a NIC (enp8s0) was likewise never given a persistent address — the executor's own probe/revert sequence (cmd-000f7197070144ed, then reverted by cmd-47c7c7f014eb4952) shows enp8s0 ended with no IPv4 address. The 'node2 reachability' evidence the executor cites (cmd-090baa37a7cb48f4, cmd-989760d59fad42f8) pings node2's mgmt-network DHCP address 10.100.217.11 over enp1s0, which sits on the same flat 10.100.217.0/24 segment as node1's vtnet0 and node3's own enp1s0 — a path that does not require any lan-a zone or routing through node1's lanb interface. No lan-a addressing, route, or NAT/pass rule tied to lan-a was ever established, so the specific instructed outcome (reaching node2 via lan-a, routed through node1) is not demonstrated; the observed ping success is explained by pre-existing mgmt-network adjacency instead.","evidence_ids":["cmd-5d4d4f39143141f2","cmd-ab142efccb84457a","cmd-000f7197070144ed","cmd-47c7c7f014eb4952","cmd-090baa37a7cb48f4","cmd-5928fe5be66246a4"]},{"id":"req-4-ssh-preserved","requirement":"Existing SSH management path on node1 preserved while applying the filter/interface changes","status":"satisfied","summary":"configctl filter reload returned OK and subsequent exec commands over the same SSH session succeeded; pfctl -s rules still shows the vtnet0 SSH pass rule intact after the change.","evidence_ids":["cmd-91de030881634621","cmd-77cf9e04e4774877"]},{"id":"req-5-node1-restart-evidence","requirement":"Provider-wide restart protocol on node1: baseline, restart ack, changed boot identity, recovered config/connectivity","status":"satisfied","summary":"Baseline kern.boottime captured (17:18:37); reboot issued and acknowledged; after a bounded series of timeouts, kern.boottime changed to 17:22:53 confirming a real reboot; post-restart check shows vtnet2/lanb and its pf pass rule intact, consistent with the fixed after_executor lifecycle snapshot's boot-identity of 17:22:53.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f30d970b0f024d84","cmd-f85505d86ab9469c","cmd-1c775c8ff4224cd2","cmd-1f1f442ba8144a85"]},{"id":"req-6-node2-restart-evidence","requirement":"Provider-wide restart protocol on node2: baseline, restart ack, changed boot identity, recovered connectivity","status":"satisfied","summary":"Baseline boot_id 31949c21 captured with healthy enp1s0 address; reboot issued (ack ambiguous, stream closed, but plausible given subsequent timeouts); new boot_id 701be57e confirms reboot; enp1s0 address recovered and ping to node1 succeeded post-restart, consistent with the fixed after_executor snapshot boot_id.","evidence_ids":["cmd-722ed685f13b46b0","cmd-6f4c739826f34484","cmd-c4f6169ace514396","cmd-7e97f12ccb414443"]},{"id":"req-7-node3-restart-evidence","requirement":"Provider-wide restart protocol on node3: baseline, restart ack, changed boot identity, recovered lan-b config and connectivity","status":"satisfied","summary":"Baseline boot_id 9feeb0bb captured; reboot issued (ack ambiguous stream closure, but followed by expected outage/timeouts); new boot_id 91d01ec7 confirms reboot; enp8s0's static 10.60.60.12/24 persisted and post-restart ping to node1 (lan-b) succeeded, consistent with the fixed after_executor snapshot boot_id. (The accompanying post-restart ping to node2 shares the same lan-a interpretation issue noted in req-3.)","evidence_ids":["cmd-ff82a2887c3840b5","cmd-f4e09659e926442d","cmd-58cd127c92a6454e","cmd-989760d59fad42f8"]}],"confidence":0.75,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.8,"summary":"The executor ran a bounded topology-discovery sequence — temporarily assigning vtnet1/vtnet2 on node1 (cmd-2bb2c82f75af4978) and matching addresses on node2's enp8s0 (cmd-000f7197070144ed) and node3's enp8s0 (cmd-d2c16499cf1541e7) — to determine which physical NIC corresponded to lan-a vs lan-b via tcpdump; all three were promptly reverted (cmd-d0c74c1a6a9b414f, cmd-47c7c7f014eb4952, cmd-80d1f3ea12d6421a) and left no residue. This was a low-risk, fully-restored discovery step tied to a legitimate prerequisite (correctly identifying which vtnet maps to lan-b) so it is not treated as a serious violation. Several `configctl interface reload`/`configctl interface reload lanb` attempts failed with 'Action not allowed or missing' (cmd-4f13a932f62640ff, cmd-b512c64d77434891, cmd-d859a4c37d5a4cbe) before the correct `configctl interface reconfigure lanb` was found — these were failed attempts with no state change and are not penalized. One unexplained residual item: the after_executor lifecycle snapshot shows a new failed unit on node2, NetworkManager-wait-online.service, that was not present in the before_prepare baseline; this emerged only after the mandatory restart and has no evidence tying it to the executor's lan-a/lan-b work, but it is a state change that was not reverted or investigated.","evidence_ids":["cmd-2bb2c82f75af4978","cmd-d2c16499cf1541e7","cmd-000f7197070144ed","cmd-d0c74c1a6a9b414f","cmd-47c7c7f014eb4952","cmd-80d1f3ea12d6421a","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:before_prepare:node2:failed-services"]},"overall_summary":"The executor correctly brought node1's unconfigured vtnet2 into service as a new 'lanb' OPNsense zone (10.60.60.1/24) with a matching pf pass rule, and directly demonstrated node3 reaching node1 over that new lan-b network after configuring node3's enp8s0 to 10.60.60.12/24. However, the third instructed outcome — node3 reaching node2 on lan-a through node1 — is not actually substantiated: neither node1's lan-a NIC (vtnet1) nor node2's lan-a NIC (enp8s0) was ever given a persistent address, so the ping the executor cites as proof instead traveled over the pre-existing flat mgmt network (10.100.217.0/24) that node1, node2, and node3 are all already directly attached to, not through any lan-a zone or node1 routing. The restart protocol was completed on all three nodes with clear before/after boot-identity evidence and recovered state, cross-consistent with the fixed lifecycle snapshots, though the reboot acknowledgements on node2 and node3 were via ambiguous exec-stream closures rather than clean command completions, and node2 shows one new failed systemd unit after restart that is unexplained.","limitations":["Reboot acknowledgements for node2 and node3 registered as 'exec stream closed before command completion' / return_code -1 rather than a clean success, so the restart trigger itself is inferred from subsequent connectivity loss and later boot_id change rather than a direct confirmation.","No routing table, traceroute, or lan-a-specific firewall/NAT evidence was captured on node1, so it cannot be independently confirmed whether any lan-a-labeled traffic ever traversed node1 versus the flat mgmt network.","The new failed unit (NetworkManager-wait-online.service) on node2 after restart has no causal evidence linking it to executor actions versus generic reboot behavior in this image."]},"ttft_ms":80482,"type":"result","duration_ms":146900,"uuid":"af79fdb3-19d6-4db3-9cc5-ff76758da082","ttft_stream_ms":2930,"time_to_request_ms":37,"first_content_frame_ms":3329,"queued_turn_count":0}
